The electric field at the midpoint due to each charge has magnitude

\[
E = \frac{kq}{r^2}
\]

where \(q = 4.0 \times 10^{-9}\,\text{C}\), \(r = \frac{2.0\,\text{cm}}{2}=1.0\,\text{cm}=0.010\,\text{m}\), and \(k \approx 9.0 \times 10^9\).

\[
E = \frac{(9.0 \times 10^9)(4.0 \times 10^{-9})}{(0.010)^2}
= \frac{36}{1.0 \times 10^{-4}}
= 3.6 \times 10^5\,\text{N/C}
\]

Since the charges are opposite, the two electric fields point in the same direction at the midpoint, so they add:

\[
E_{\text{total}} = 2(3.6 \times 10^5)
= 7.2 \times 10^5\,\text{N/C}
\]

